.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt CHKCONFIG 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm chkconfig
.Nd decide which services a runlevel starts
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -list
.Op Ar name
.Nm
.Fl -add Ar name
.Nm
.Fl -del Ar name
.Nm
.Op Fl -level Ar levels
.Ar name Cm on | off | reset
.Nm
.Ar name
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc Ns Ar N Ns Pa .d
directories are the real configuration.
A service is started in a runlevel because there is an
.Pa S Ns Ar NN Ns Ar name
link for it there, and stopped because there is a
.Pa K Ns Ar NN Ns Ar name
link;
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc
runs the K links and then the S links, in the order the two-digit priorities
give.
.Nm
is how those links are made without counting digits by hand.
.Pp
The defaults come from the init script's own header, which every Sowa init
script carries:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# chkconfig: 2345 50 40
# description: what the service is
.Ed
.Pp
The three fields are the runlevels the service belongs in -
.Ql -
for none - and the two-digit start and stop priorities.
Every runlevel ends up with a link either way: S where the service runs, K
where it must be stopped if it is running.
A script with no such header is refused rather than guessed at.
.Pp
With a
.Ar name
and no action,
.Nm
asks whether the service is started in the current runlevel and answers with
its exit status, which is what a script wants.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--level levels"
.It Fl -list Op Ar name
Print the state of every service in every runlevel, or of one service.
.It Fl -add Ar name
Make the links the script's header asks for: S in the runlevels it names, K in
all the others.
This is what a package does when it installs a service.
.It Fl -del Ar name
Remove every link for the service, in every runlevel.
The init script is left alone.
A service removed this way is neither started nor stopped by a runlevel change,
which is not the same as
.Cm off .
.It Fl -level Ar levels
Apply
.Cm on
or
.Cm off
to these runlevels only, written together with no separator:
.Ql 35 .
.It Cm on
Start the service in the runlevels its header names, or in those given by
.Fl -level ,
and stop it in the rest.
A header that names none is taken as 2345.
.It Cm off
Do not start it in any runlevel, or in those given by
.Fl -level .
The K links stay, so a service started by hand is still stopped on the way to a
stopped system.
.It Cm reset
Put every runlevel back to what the script's header says.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/rc.d/rcN.d" -compact
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d
the init scripts, and where the headers are read from.
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/rc Ns Ar N Ns Pa .d
the links this command makes, for runlevels 0 to 6.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Nm Ar name
with no action exits 0 when the service is started in the current runlevel and
1 when it is not.
.Sh EXAMPLES
What starts where:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ chkconfig --list
chronyd             0:off 1:off 2:on  3:on  4:on  5:on  6:off
crond               0:off 1:off 2:on  3:on  4:on  5:on  6:off
openvpn             0:off 1:off 2:off 3:off 4:off 5:off 6:off
.Ed
.Pp
Turn a shipped-off service on at boot, and start it now -
.Nm
does not:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
chkconfig wg-quick on
service wg-quick start
.Ed
.Pp
Stop starting cron at boot, without stopping the one that is running:
.Pp
.Dl chkconfig crond off
.Pp
Only in the two multi-user runlevels this machine uses:
.Pp
.Dl chkconfig --level 35 named on
.Pp
Ask, from a script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if chkconfig sshd; then ...
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr inittab 5 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr runlevel 8 ,
.Xr service 8
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
changes what the next runlevel change does and nothing about the machine as it
is now.
A service is started and stopped with
.Xr service 8 .
